kubernetes is great for running a lot of  different containers at scale but those  containers contain code that is  constantly changing  and when there's a lot of change there's  risk how can you reduce this risk  involved with dirty software deployments  to kubernetes  hi my name is barbara moros and i'm a  customer engineer at google  today we'll talk about how to reduce  this risk using the release pattern of  canary deployments and introduce the  ways you can put this into practice in  gk  [Music]  when releasing new code there are  different deployment strategies that can  look like canary deployments but are in  practice different  it's important to review this as each  has their own benefit and considerations  with a shadow strategy you deployed a  new version of the application alongside  the existing version of the application  instead of users getting responses from  this new version however requests are  instead duplicated in other words  sending mirror but ultimately synthetic  traffic to this new version  with this approach while your users may  not directly interact with the new  version you do need to manage complexity  such as ensuring actions that our  requests can kick off are item potent  and don't affect real downstream  services  a b testing is another deployment  strategy mostly used to make business  decisions you test the hypothesis by  routing a specific subset of users to a  version with new functionality you then  monitor user behavior within your app  and make the decision to promote the  version with the best results according  to your desired user behavior  with a canary version you test a new  change by deploying a new version of  your application alongside the  production version  then you split the production traffic  where a small subset of users will be  using the canary version  now you can monitor the performance of  the canary version  this test pattern mitigates some risks  of introducing new versions to  production since it allows you to  collect relevant data and potentially  early warning signs around your new  version's performance  then you can decide whether to make it  available to older users or not if the  changes are not satisfactory you can  easily roll back by redirecting traffic  to the older versions  so how does this testing pattern work in  kubernetes  let's walk through an example let's say  we have a stable version of an app  serving the entire user base exposed via  kubernetes service  then you deploy the new version of your  application which we'll call the canary  version alongside your stable deployment  except with a much smaller number of  replicas  this canary version will have the same  labels that match the selectors that the  kubernetes service uses  only a small amount of traffic will go  to the canary version as there are fewer  replicas  you might be thinking that it is best to  just compare the canary deployment  against the current production  deployment  however we recommend that you compare  the canary deployment with a baseline  that is the same version as a stable  deployment but deploy alongside the  canary with the same number of replicas  this helps alleviate any performance  advantages the existing stable replicas  may have from having been up for longer  such as warm caches  before you can promote the canary too  stable by scaling it up and tearing down  the old baseline and all stable versions  you need to determine what metrics  you'll monitor to help you make the  decision to promote the canary  this could be metrics such as response  latency or cpu usage  once the metrics collected and reviewed  are adequate by your measure you can  gradually scale up the canary version  until it is the only version running  this approach has some challenges  however  controlling the amount of traffic your  canary version gets by managing replicas  can be challenging especially if your  stable version has auto scalers involved  it's an imperative way to implement  canary rollouts how can we achieve the  same in a declarative manner  to do this in a declarative manner you  can look to utilize load balancer or  service mesh technology to directly  reference the amount of traffic you'd  like to be sent across various versions  of your application  by declaring this the load balancer or  service mesh will handle ensuring that  each person gets a specific percentage  of traffic regardless of auto scaling  that might be in place  canary deployments are a notable pattern  to adopt in gke for your workloads you  can quickly roll out new versions of  apps with the ability to test those  versions live production traffic and  roll back quickly should your users  encounter any issues  stay tuned for our next video where  we'll demonstrate how to achieve this in  decay with both of the mechanisms  discussed today  [Music]  you
 

